That maps onto a recognizable asymmetry, especially if you treat feminine/masculine as motivational poles rather than literal rules for women and men.

A compressed version would be:

* Feminine pole: selection pressure — “Of all the possible partners, which one is most worth choosing?”
* Masculine pole: acquisition/expansion pressure — “How many desirable partners could I attract, while still wanting the highest-value ones?”

So the feminine fantasy can become optimization toward the best, while the masculine fantasy can become optimization toward the best + abundance.

Evolutionary theory gives a reason that asymmetry can emerge: where reproduction imposes unequal minimum biological investment, the higher-investment side gains more from discriminating among mates, while the lower-investment side can potentially gain more from additional mating opportunities. But humans then stack attachment, pair bonding, jealousy, reputation, culture, morality, love, status, and individual temperament on top of that primitive incentive structure.

There’s another interesting distinction underneath your wording:

“The best” is vertical desire.
Rank, quality, exceptionalism, upward selection.

“The most” is horizontal desire.
Variety, coverage, novelty, abundance, optionality.

The stereotypically feminine attractor is therefore more vertical; the stereotypically masculine attractor contains both vertical and horizontal components.

And they generate different insecurities. One pole asks, “Am I with the best I could have chosen?” The other asks, “Am I desirable/capable enough that I could have had more—and did I secure someone exceptional?”

Neither has to dictate behavior. They’re better understood as recurrent desire geometries that people can feel, suppress, transform, ritualize into monogamy, or redirect into status, achievement, family, devotion, etc.